---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { capabilities, integrations, statusLabel } from "../../shared/capability";
import { faqs, useCases, workflowStages } from "../../shared/site";

const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Organization",
    name: "Cyber Pirate Labs, LLC",
    url: "https://cyberpiratelabs.com",
    logo: "https://cyberpiratelabs.com/brand/cpl-logo.png",
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    name: "CPL Command Center",
    applicationCategory: "BusinessApplication",
    operatingSystem: "Web",
    creativeWorkStatus: "Early access. In development.",
    description: "Configurable workflow software for service companies. Early access, not generally available.",
    provider: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Cyber Pirate Labs, LLC" },
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="CPL Command Center | Cyber Pirate Labs"
  description="CPL Command Center connects requests, proposals, scheduling, field updates, and reporting for service companies. Early access. Not generally available."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="hero" id="product">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">CPL Command Center</p>
        <h1>Turn scattered work into a connected operation.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Requests, proposals, scheduling, field updates, and reporting can live in one workflow. Command Center is configurable for a service company. It is in early access, not a finished hosted product.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button light" href="/contact/?interest=demonstration">Request a Demo</a>
          <a class="button light secondary" href="/demo/">Explore the Interactive Demo</a>
        </div>
        <p class="notice">The picture on the right is a product story with legible steps. It is not a live customer system, and it does not require an account.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-card" aria-label="Sample path from request to clear status">
        <ol>
          <li><span class="step-index">1</span><div><strong>Request arrives</strong><span>Roof assessment, 18 Cedar Wharf, requested 18 April.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="step-index">2</span><div><strong>Information organized</strong><span>Site, scope, and date sit on one intake record.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="step-index">3</span><div><strong>Gap surfaced</strong><span>Access notes are missing. A person has to supply them.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="step-index">4</span><div><strong>Next task prepared</strong><span>A sample proposal is waiting for review.</span></div></li>
          <li><span class="step-index">5</span><div><strong>Status clear</strong><span>The record says what is blocked, and what happens next.</span></div></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The operational problem</p>
      <h2>Work is already happening. It is just not connected.</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <article class="panel-block">
          <h3>Before</h3>
          <p>The request is in a mailbox. The photos are in a thread. The approval is in someone’s head. The schedule is on a board. The customer asks for a status that nobody can answer from one place.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="panel-block">
          <h3>After</h3>
          <p>The request becomes a record. Missing information stays visible. A person approves the next step. The following task is prepared. The status is something the team can point to.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-muted" id="how-it-works">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">A connected workflow</p>
      <h2>Select a step. See what moves, and what waits for a person.</h2>
      <p>The sequence is configurable. A company does not have to use every step. Labels below separate the development source from this sample.</p>
      <div class="explorer">
        {workflowStages.map((stage, index) => (
          <>
            <input class="stage-input" type="radio" name="stage" id={`stage-${stage.id}`} checked={index === 0} />
            <label class="stage-label" for={`stage-${stage.id}`}>{stage.title}</label>
          </>
        ))}
        {workflowStages.map((stage) => (
          <article class={`stage-panel panel-${stage.id}`}>
            <h3>{stage.title}</h3>
            <dl class="facts">
              <dt>Input</dt><dd>{stage.input}</dd>
              <dt>Organized</dt><dd>{stage.organized}</dd>
              <dt>A person</dt><dd>{stage.human}</dd>
              <dt>Outcome</dt><dd>{stage.outcome}</dd>
            </dl>
            <p class="evidence">{stage.evidence}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Hands-on preview</p>
      <h2>Run a sample. No email gate.</h2>
      <p>The interactive walkthrough uses invented companies and deterministic rules. It is labeled sample data. It does not read a mailbox or call a model.</p>
      <p><a class="button" href="/demo/">Open the interactive demo</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-muted" id="use-cases">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Use cases</p>
      <h2>Three ways work gets stuck.</h2>
      {useCases.map((item) => (
        <article class="case">
          <p class="kicker">{item.title}</p>
          <div>
            <h3>{item.problem}</h3>
            <p><strong>Workflow. </strong>{item.workflow}</p>
            <p><strong>Outcome. </strong>{item.outcome}</p>
          </div>
          <a class="button secondary" href={item.href}>Try this scenario</a>
        </article>
      ))}
      <p class="evidence">Field service and recurring property care are illustrative scenarios on this website. The inspection path is aligned to stage shapes in the public development source, and it is still a sample.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Views</p>
      <h2>The same job, from three seats.</h2>
      <p class="evidence">Illustrative. These panels are a website composition, not screenshots of a hosted product.</p>
      <div class="views">
        <article>
          <h3>Internal operating view</h3>
          <p>The record shows the request, the gap, and the next task. A coordinator can see what is unassigned.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Review queue</h3>
          <p>Approvals sit in a queue. A proposal or an exception waits until a person decides. The queue does not decide for them.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Customer status</h3>
          <p>The customer-facing line says what is known and what is not released. The sample does not send it.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-muted" id="integrations">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Integrations</p>
      <h2>Configured per company. Not a wall of connected badges.</h2>
      <p>Nothing in this list is live on this website. Command Center does not replace every CRM, accounting package, or field tool.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>State</th><th>What that means</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {integrations.map((item) => (
              <tr><td>{item.name}</td><td>{item.state}</td><td>{item.note}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="implementation">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Implementation</p>
      <h2>Configure the platform. Do not rebuild it for every company.</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <article class="panel-block">
          <h3>How a company starts</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Understand the workflow that is actually stuck.</li>
            <li>Configure the steps the company needs.</li>
            <li>Test that path with the team.</li>
            <li>Launch, then improve the configuration.</li>
          </ol>
        </article>
        <article class="panel-block">
          <h3>Commercial shape</h3>
          <p>One reusable platform. Company-specific onboarding, workflow configuration, and integrations. Implementation and configuration fees, plus an ongoing subscription.</p>
          <p>Prices, dates, and contract terms are not listed here. They are part of a discussion.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-muted" id="availability">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">What is available today</p>
      <h2>Early access. The boundary is written down.</h2>
      <p>Public copy on this site follows this list. The notes refer to the public Command Center source reviewed on 2026-09-21. They do not claim a hosted production service.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>Status</th><th>Plain meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {capabilities.map((item) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{item.name}</td>
                <td>{statusLabel(item.status)}</td>
                <td>{item.publicMeaning}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">FAQ</p>
      <h2>Straight answers.</h2>
      {faqs.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.q}</summary>
          <p>{item.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="final">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Next step</p>
          <h2>Show us where work gets stuck.</h2>
          <p>Tell us the workflow. Requesting a demo does not book a time.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="button light" href="/contact/?interest=implementation">Request a Demo</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
